## Proof

We claim the only entire function satisfying $f(x) = e^x$ for all real $x$ is $f(z) = e^z$.

**Setup.** Let $g(z) = f(z) - e^z$. Since $f$ is entire and $e^z$ is entire, $g$ is entire. For every real number $x$,
$$g(x) = f(x) - e^x = 0.$$
Thus $g$ vanishes on the entire real line $\mathbb{R}$.

**Key fact (Identity Theorem).** If an analytic function on a domain $\Omega$ vanishes on a subset $S \subseteq \Omega$ that has an accumulation point in $\Omega$, then the function is identically zero on $\Omega$.

**Application.** The real line $\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of $\mathbb{C}$ with accumulation points (every real number is an accumulation point of $\mathbb{R}$, e.g. $0$ is the limit of the sequence $1/n \in \mathbb{R}$). Since $g$ is entire (analytic on all of $\mathbb{C}$) and vanishes on $\mathbb{R}$, which has an accumulation point in $\mathbb{C}$, the Identity Theorem gives
$$g(z) \equiv 0 \quad \text{for all } z \in \mathbb{C}.$$

**Conclusion.** Therefore $f(z) = e^z$ for all $z \in \mathbb{C}$.

**Verification.** The function $f(z) = e^z$ is entire, and for real $x$, $f(x) = e^x$, so it satisfies the condition. By the argument above, it is the unique such function.

The only entire function $f$ with $f(x) = e^x$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$ is $\boxed{f(z) = e^z}$.

